/*****************************************************************************
    parseClass -- the setParse CLASSIFICATION CENSUS.

    For every rule reachable from Grokking, and for every term of every rule,
    this binds a parse method and prints which arm of setParse claimed it. It
    answers one question and answers it before any parse happens: when the walk
    binds this grammar, what will each field be executed by.

    WHY IT EXISTS. Both parse generation defects found on 2026-08-19 were
    classification defects, and neither needed a parse to be visible. tokenize
    was falling to the data switch and binding parseString, which before the
    parseString repair reported success without matching anything. CodE, which
    the grammar declares parseAction, came within one arm order of binding
    parseRule instead. A table like this shows both at a glance and nothing in
    the fleet showed either.

    THE STOP IS INSIDE THE DRIVER ACTION, AND THAT IS THE SHAPE NOT A STYLE.
    Binding parse methods across the grammar poisons the loader, so the parser
    can no longer read the source that follows. An action body is parsed once at
    define time, so a driver that never returns to the loader is the only shape
    that survives its own effect.

    EVERY LINE IS ON CERR for the same reason the walk fixtures are: stdout is
    block buffered and a run that ends badly loses it.

    ONE SENTINEL, AT THE FOOT, INSIDE THE DRIVER.
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
search reset stack Grokking;
search UnitTests list;
register(ParseClass);
define
    /*  THE SKIPS ARE walkPhase's, DELIBERATELY COPIED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.
        A census that walks where the real walk does not reports its own
        shape, not the grammar's. Without these it descended into the
        Operators registry and produced 54 rows of `NO-rSTUFF` for `+`, `==`,
        `AND` and their siblings -- container entries the walk never binds,
        and 54 setParse errors this fixture invented for itself.
        ⚠ THE SKIP IS ON THE DESCENT, NOT ON THE CLASSIFICATION. A container
        TERM is a real term and wants parseContainer; it is descending into
        its ENTRIES that is wrong. Skipping both was the first correction and
        it silently deleted every parseContainer row -- an over-correction
        that reads exactly like a clean result.  */
    pcTerms argument code={
        iterate pcA on argument attributes;
        while ++pcA;
            if noPrinT;     continue;
            setParse(pcA);
            parseClassify(pcA);
        iterate pcM on argument members;
        while ++pcM;
            if noPrinT;     continue;
            setParse(pcM);
            parseClassify(pcM);
        };
    pcWalk argument code={
        iterate pcR on argument members;
        while ++pcR;
            if noPrinT;     continue;
            if !isRulE;     continue;
            setParse(pcR);
            parseClassify(pcR);
            if binTypE;     continue;
            pcTerms(pcR);
        };
    pcRun code={
        cerr "== PARSE CLASSIFICATION CENSUS ==":;
        pcWalk(Grokking);
        cerr "PARSECLASS SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
        stop();
        };
    ;
pcRun();
